WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:07.800
Every mine uses chemicals, and ours are no different.

00:07.800 --> 00:10.425
At our sites, that list includes diesel,

00:10.425 --> 00:13.775
motor and hydraulic oils, grease, antifreeze and

00:13.775 --> 00:17.363
coolant, solvents and degreasers, welding gases,

00:17.363 --> 00:20.775
and blasting products like ANFO and packaged emulsion.

00:20.775 --> 00:23.062
Most of these feel ordinary because you handle

00:23.062 --> 00:26.363
them every day. That's exactly why they hurt people.

00:26.363 --> 00:28.538
A safety data sheet tells you what a chemical can

00:28.538 --> 00:31.637
do to you and what to do about it. This video shows

00:31.637 --> 00:34.312
you how to find one and how to read it in a hurry.

00:35.436 --> 00:39.524
The rule behind this is Part 47, hazard communication.

00:39.524 --> 00:42.424
Each operator must have a written HazCom program

00:42.424 --> 00:44.736
and keep it as long as a hazardous chemical is

00:44.736 --> 00:47.661
at the mine. At Allen, the written program is kept

00:47.661 --> 00:50.586
on Dropbox, and the safety manager is responsible

00:50.586 --> 00:54.061
for it. The paper SDS binder is in the breakroom

00:54.061 --> 00:57.199
at every site. You have a right to see any of it.

00:57.199 --> 01:01.699
Just ask. Part 47 still calls the sheet an MSDS,

01:01.699 --> 01:04.611
a material safety data sheet. Suppliers now call

01:04.611 --> 01:07.186
it an SDS. It's the same idea.

01:08.310 --> 01:11.160
At Boonesboro, for example, the sheets are kept

01:11.160 --> 01:14.423
in the yellow SDS station on the breakroom wall,

01:14.423 --> 01:16.785
right under the first aid cabinet. Find yours on

01:16.785 --> 01:17.873
your first day.

01:18.997 --> 01:21.947
Before you ever open the binder, read the label.

01:21.947 --> 01:24.372
Every container of a hazardous chemical has to

01:24.372 --> 01:28.284
be labeled, and nobody may remove or deface a label.

01:28.284 --> 01:30.797
If a label is missing or you can't read it,

01:30.797 --> 01:33.297
report it so it can be replaced right away.

01:33.297 --> 01:35.084
If you pour something into a bucket,

01:35.084 --> 01:37.997
a spray bottle or a can, that container needs a

01:37.997 --> 01:41.047
label too. An unlabeled jug of clear liquid is

01:41.047 --> 01:44.172
a guess, and guessing is how people get burned.

01:47.482 --> 01:50.632
A safety data sheet has sixteen numbered sections,

01:50.632 --> 01:53.532
always in the same order. That's good news,

01:53.532 --> 01:56.370
because once you learn one, you can read them all.

01:56.370 --> 01:59.345
You don't need to read all sixteen to stay safe.

01:59.345 --> 02:02.757
Start with four: section one, identification;

02:02.757 --> 02:05.920
section two, hazards; section four,

02:05.920 --> 02:09.232
first aid; and section eight, exposure controls

02:09.232 --> 02:11.832
and personal protection. We'll walk through a generic

02:11.832 --> 02:15.470
hydraulic oil sheet, a product we use at our sites.

02:15.470 --> 02:18.057
It's an example only. Always read the sheet for

02:18.057 --> 02:20.020
the actual product at your site.

02:21.161 --> 02:23.711
Section one tells you what the product is and who

02:23.711 --> 02:26.473
made it. Match the product name on the sheet to

02:26.473 --> 02:28.986
the name on the label. If they don't match,

02:28.986 --> 02:31.661
you're reading the wrong sheet. This section also

02:31.661 --> 02:34.323
lists the supplier and an emergency phone number

02:34.323 --> 02:35.348
for the product.

02:36.491 --> 02:39.666
Section two is the most important page in the sheet.

02:39.666 --> 02:42.191
It gives the signal word, either Danger,

02:42.191 --> 02:45.666
the more severe, or Warning. It shows the pictograms,

02:45.666 --> 02:48.503
and it lists hazard statements in plain words.

02:48.503 --> 02:51.153
Many hydraulic oil sheets have no signal word at

02:51.153 --> 02:54.816
all, because the oil isn't classified as hazardous.

02:54.816 --> 02:56.966
That doesn't mean it's harmless. Read the other

02:56.966 --> 03:00.303
sections too. Oil in a hydraulic system is hot

03:00.303 --> 03:02.953
and under high pressure, and a pinhole leak can

03:02.953 --> 03:05.941
inject oil through your skin. Never run your hand

03:05.941 --> 03:09.503
along a hose to find a leak. Spilled oil also makes

03:09.503 --> 03:12.303
floors and steps slick. Check the sheet for your

03:12.303 --> 03:13.541
actual product.

03:14.656 --> 03:16.881
Section four tells you what to do if someone is

03:16.881 --> 03:19.756
exposed. Our first aid follows the American Heart

03:19.756 --> 03:22.444
Association. For chemicals on the skin,

03:22.444 --> 03:25.256
take off contaminated clothing and rinse with plenty

03:25.256 --> 03:28.256
of running water. For the eyes, rinse with lots

03:28.256 --> 03:30.531
of water, at least fifteen minutes,

03:30.531 --> 03:33.944
using the nearest eyewash. For hydraulic oil,

03:33.944 --> 03:36.456
a typical sheet says that oil injected under the

03:36.456 --> 03:39.456
skin by high pressure is a medical emergency,

03:39.456 --> 03:42.456
even if the wound looks small, so get medical help

03:42.456 --> 03:45.456
right away. Get medical help following our emergency

03:45.456 --> 03:47.994
procedure, and bring the sheet or the label with

03:47.994 --> 03:50.619
you so the medical people know exactly what it

03:50.619 --> 03:51.406
was.

03:52.522 --> 03:55.185
Section eight tells you how to protect yourself.

03:55.185 --> 03:58.360
It lists exposure limits, the ventilation needed,

03:58.360 --> 04:01.135
and the protective equipment: which type of gloves,

04:01.135 --> 04:03.422
what eye protection, and whether a respirator is

04:03.422 --> 04:06.222
needed under certain conditions. Not every glove

04:06.222 --> 04:09.010
stops every chemical, and the sheet tells you which

04:09.010 --> 04:11.622
kind does. If the sheet calls for equipment you

04:11.622 --> 04:14.585
don't have, stop and ask your supervisor before

04:14.585 --> 04:15.485
you start.

04:18.812 --> 04:21.550
When you have a minute, read a few more sections.

04:21.550 --> 04:24.637
Section five covers firefighting: which extinguisher

04:24.637 --> 04:27.087
works and what the smoke can contain.

04:27.087 --> 04:30.962
Our sites have ABC and carbon dioxide extinguishers.

04:30.962 --> 04:34.175
Section six covers spills: how to contain it,

04:34.175 --> 04:36.337
what to keep it away from, and what protection

04:36.337 --> 04:39.150
to wear while you clean it up. Section seven covers

04:39.150 --> 04:42.387
handling and storage, like keeping containers closed

04:42.387 --> 04:44.925
and away from heat and open flame.

04:46.046 --> 04:48.984
Sections nine through sixteen are more technical.

04:48.984 --> 04:51.596
They cover physical properties, stability,

04:51.596 --> 04:55.059
toxicology, environmental effects, disposal,

04:55.059 --> 04:58.771
transport and regulations. Section sixteen usually

04:58.771 --> 05:01.446
shows the revision date. You don't need to memorize

05:01.446 --> 05:03.671
them, but know they're there if a doctor or your

05:03.671 --> 05:05.233
supervisor asks.

05:08.569 --> 05:11.857
Here's a scenario. A degreaser splashes on a mechanic's

05:11.857 --> 05:14.632
forearm in the shop. What comes first?

05:14.632 --> 05:17.082
Rinse it with plenty of running water right away.

05:17.082 --> 05:19.682
Then someone grabs the sheet for that exact product

05:19.682 --> 05:22.232
from the binder in the breakroom and checks section

05:22.232 --> 05:25.644
four for anything more. Report it to your supervisor,

05:25.644 --> 05:27.106
even if it seems minor.

05:28.218 --> 05:31.130
Another one. You find an unlabeled jug of clear

05:31.130 --> 05:34.593
liquid on a bench. Do you use it? No. Don't sniff

05:34.593 --> 05:36.843
it to find out, and don't pour it out.

05:36.843 --> 05:39.580
Leave it, and report it to your supervisor so it

05:39.580 --> 05:41.543
can be identified and labeled.

05:42.660 --> 05:45.935
Let's wrap up. Read the label before you use anything.

05:45.935 --> 05:48.322
The SDS binder is in the breakroom,

05:48.322 --> 05:51.160
and the written HazCom program is on Dropbox,

05:51.160 --> 05:53.447
kept by the safety manager. When you look at a

05:53.447 --> 05:58.098
sheet, read sections one, two, four and eight first.

05:58.098 --> 06:00.035
And if you're not sure what something is or how

06:00.035 --> 06:02.910
to protect yourself, stop and ask. Your site's

06:02.910 --> 06:05.460
plans always override this video.

06:06.582 --> 06:09.745
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

06:09.745 --> 06:11.245
override this video.
